 {"id": "JEV-404", "title": "[P1] escalation storm: 78 of 80 cycles escalate on 'insufficient_state (0.38-0.48) missing=none', so the 2-attempt per-class allowance can never bind", "description": "Dogfood use 2026-11-20, run df1120a (80 cycles, --decision-mode jev). Every one of the 78 teacher_escalation rows carries pre_ask.escalate_reason = 'insufficient_state (<suff>) missing=none' with suff between 0.38 and 0.48. The reason string is self-contradictory for a user reading the run: it says the state is insufficient AND that nothing is missing. 36 of those 78 escalations did not improve anything (run summary: 'teacher=78 escalations (42 improved)'). Mechanism in code: cron_runner.py:486-491 declares TEACHER_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS_PER_CLASS = 2 and TEACHER_NON_CLASS_SENTINELS = frozenset({'none'}) — the 'none' sentinel is deliberately EXEMPT from the per-class allowance (the comment explains why: treating it as one shared class let confidence/failure/gap triggers exhaust each other). The consequence is unbounded repetition: because a sufficiency-floor escalation names no class, it never consumes an allowance, so nothing bounds it (cron_runner.py:3657-3665 applies the cap only to real classes). Observed effect on wall clock and cost is filed separately as PERF-8 (556.97s of a 611.90s run, $0.1236). This row is the policy defect, not the timing.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Give the sufficiency-floor escalation a bounded identity: either name the missing class from the triggering signal (gap/confidence/failure family from HANDOFF_ALL_FAMILIES) when suff < SUFFICIENCY_FLOOR, or count the 'none' sentinel against its own named allowance instead of exempting it. 2) Proof after fix: an 80-cycle jev run on the S0 boot state shows teacher_escalation count under 20 in the run log that command writes (record its path in worker_summary; currently 78) with autonomy not worse than 58/80 and no new 'missing=none' escalations beyond the allowance. 3) Unit test: an escalation whose reason is 'insufficient_state ... missing=none' must be refused once its allowance is spent, and the refusal must appear in the run log with the named block reason constant (HANDOFF_BLOCK_*). The test path goes in worker_summary. 4) Keep the DF-AIPP-13-R2 comment's requirement (findable in code, greppable constants) — do not inline a string where a grep-able constant exists.\n\nGATE ARM: run `python3 cron_runner.py --run-id jev404 --cycles 80 --decision-mode jev`; expect exit 0 and a teacher_escalation count under 20 in the run log named in worker_summary.", "capability_tags": ["jev", "teacher", "cost"], "complexity": 4, "created_at": "2026-11-20T10:25:53.000043Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "", "metadata": {}}, "foreman_note": null, "priority": "P1", "reasoning": "The state-projection sufficiency score is persistently below the floor (0.38-0.48) while the gap detector reports nothing missing, so the escalation gate fires on almost every cycle. Because 'none' is a non-class sentinel exempt from the repair allowance, the 2-attempt cap that was meant to stop exactly this repetition cannot see these escalations. 10-27 baseline (DF-AIPP-19) finished the same 80 cycles in 49.40s (0.62 s/cycle) with autonomy_ratio 0.925 (74/80 jev); today's identical command takes 611.90s, so the escalation rate — not navigation alone — is what changed the cost. Also worth flagging to whoever fixes the accounting: the run summary's own line contradicts the log — it prints 'autonomy=58/80 (58 escalated)' while the log holds 78 teacher_escalation rows, so a user cannot tell which number is the escalation count.", "source": null, "status": "complete", "type": "bug", "updated_at": "2026-11-21T02:21:34Z", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/ai_plays_poke-JEV-404", "branch": "wt/JEV-404", "primary_model": "openai/gpt-6.1-terra", "primary_provider": "xkiro", "worker_status": "verified", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-21T06:10:00Z", "commit_hash": "bcc61b683503530074aa3a070f3aae9cd1773aba", "evidence_commit": "