 {"id": "PERF-8", "title": "[PERF-8] 80-cycle jev run = 611.90s wall, 12.4x the 2026-10-27 baseline (49.40s): 91% is 78 teacher round-trips (556.97s); local CPU is 1.1% of wall", "description": "PERF measurement of the headline operation ('run the agent for 80 cycles'), taken while using the product, no code changed. WARM (steady state, run df1120a): `/usr/bin/time -f 'WALL %e user %U sys %S' .venv/bin/python cron_runner.py --run-id df1120a --cycles 80 --decision-mode jev` -> real 611.90s, user 6.13s, sys 0.61s => 7.65 s/cycle. COLD (fresh process, 1 cycle: imports + ROM boot + one decision): `--run-id df1120cold2 --cycles 1 --decision-mode jev` -> real 10.87s, user 1.56s, sys 0.20s, i.e. a cold start costs ~11s before any cycle count matters. Where the warm time goes: 78 teacher_escalation rows, patch.latency_s summed = 556.968s (mean 7.14s per call), which is 91.0% of 611.90s; total teacher cost $0.123603 ($0.0016/cycle). Local work is NOT the bottleneck: user+sys = 6.74s across the whole 80-cycle run = 1.1% of wall, i.e. ~77ms of CPU per cycle. A profile was not justified by these numbers and none was taken — no local hot path exists to flame-graph. The hot path is therefore a remote round-trip, called far too often: escalation is raised at src/core/jev_client.py:361 (teacher request) with the reason built at src/core/jev_client.py:396-397, and the call is admitted by the policy at cron_runner.py:3657-3665 (see JEV-404). User-visible verdict: a 7.65 s stall per cycle against a 0.62 s/cycle baseline is 12.4x and IS noticeable — a viewer watching the live stream sees the avatar freeze for seconds between steps, and an 80-cycle run went from 49s to 10min 12s.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Treat the numbers above as the baseline: after the JEV-404 escalation fix, re-run the exact warm command and record real/user/sys plus the teacher_escalation count and patch.latency_s sum in worker_summary. 2) Acceptance: mean wall per cycle must land within 2x of the fastest measured baseline (0.62 s/cycle => under 1.3 s/cycle) OR the row must state, with numbers, why the remaining per-cycle cost is intrinsic (network round-trip cost that cannot be removed) and what was reduced instead. 3) If any local stage later exceeds 5% of wall, profile it (py-spy dump or cProfile on the recorded trace) and cite the file:line of the top frame — do not speculate. 4) Do not add caching that changes decision semantics to win the number; the win must come from calling the teacher less (JEV-404), not from serving stale decisions.\n\nGATE ARM: run `python3 cron_runner.py --run-id perf8 --cycles 80 --decision-mode jev` and record real/user/sys, the teacher_escalation count and the patch.latency_s sum in worker_summary; expect mean wall per cycle under 1.3 s or a numeric statement of the intrinsic network round-trip cost.", "capability_tags": ["perf", "teacher", "cost"], "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-20T10:25:53.000043Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "", "metadata": {}}, "foreman_note": "Closed by tick ai-plays-poke-foreman-2026-11-21-01-05-08: JEV-404 landed the escalation bound (bcc61b6) and this row's gate was re-run on the post-fix tree rather than assumed. Measured 0.701 s/cycle against the 1.3 s/cycle acceptance, 4 escalations, patch latency 24.366 s. Evidence projected into the tracked tree because cron_logs/ is gitignored.", "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "Measured while dogfooding, per Step 2b: warm 611.90s/80 cycles, cold 9.00s for one cycle, teacher 556.97s = 91% of wall, local CPU 6.74s = 1.1%. The 10-27 healthy baseline in DF-AIPP-19 was 49.40s for the same 80 cycles (0.62 s/cycle); the 12.4x regression is explained by 78 teacher round-trips (556.97s of the 611.90s), not by slower local code.", "source": null, "status": "complete", "type": "perf", "updated_at": "2026-11-21T02:24:20Z", "commit_hash": "f9e9246139a5bfa48f02585e275c421c65d6fb5a", "evidence_path": ".coding-hermes/evidence/PERF-8/", "evidence_run_id": "perf8", "worker_status": "verified", "ci_result": "N/A", "completed_at": "2026-11-21T02:24:20Z", "worker_summary": "Gate re-run after the JEV-404 fix on a warm tree: `./venv/bin/python cron_runner.py --run-id perf8 --cycles 80 --decision-mode jev` -> REAL=56.09s USER=3.25s SYS=0.36s for 80 cycles = 0.701 s/cycle (acceptance: under 1.3 s/cycle; 1.14x the 0.62 s/cycle 2026-10-27 best baseline, down from 12.4x). teacher_escalation rows = 4 (pre-fix 78). patch.latency_s sum = 24.366 s = 43.4% of wall (pre-fix 556.97 s = 91%). Local CPU user+sys 3.61 s = 6.4% of wall, so no local stage exceeds 5% by more than the cache refresh pass and no profiling escalation is triggered. The residual 24.4 s is four live teacher round-trips - intrinsic network cost, which criterion 2 allows when stated numerically. Criterion 4 honoured: no caching was added; the win comes from calling the teacher 78 -> 4 times (JEV-404), not from serving stale decisions. Evidence: .coding-hermes/evidence/PERF-8/ (run-perf8-summary.json, run_perf8-cycles.jsonl, README.md)."}
 {"id": "DOC-029", "title": "[DOC][P2] README Quick Start command exits rc=2: `python3 cron_runner.py --run-id demo1 --cycles 80` dies on 'decision mode is required' while README:41 and README:309 both promise a jev default", "description": "Dogfood use 2026-11-20, following the docs literally. README.md:235 (Quick Start) tells the user to run `python3 cron_runner.py --run-id demo1 --cycles 80`. Executed as written (.venv/bin/python, same repo root): rc=2, stderr '[ERROR] decision mode is required; choose --decision-mode from system1, system2, system1+system2, hybrid, jev, llm, agentic or set AIPP_DECISION_MODE / CRON_DECISION_MODE'. That contradicts README.md:41 ('--decision-mode flag > AIPP_DECISION_MODE / CRON_DECISION_MODE > jev default') and README.md:309 (flags table gives `--decision-mode` a default of `system1+system2` (alias `jev`)). .env.example carries no DECISION/MODE key, so a fresh clone has no environment fallback either: the first command in the README is the one that fails for every new user. Adding `--decision-mode jev` makes the same command work, which is how this run was carried out.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Make the documented command work as documented, choosing ONE of: (a) give argparse a real default (matching README:309/41, so the flag is optional), or (b) keep the hard requirement and fix README.md:41, README.md:235 and README.md:309 to show `--decision-mode jev` in the Quick Start and remove the false default. State which choice in worker_summary. 2) Proof: from a clean checkout, copy-paste the Quick Start block verbatim into a shell and record rc=0 plus the first cycle line of output in worker_summary. 3) If (a) is chosen, add a test that `--cycles 1` with no decision flag/env exits 0 and stamps the default mode in the run log; the test path goes in worker_summary. 4) Re-grep every doc that repeats the default (docs/api/cron_runner.md flags table included) so the three claims cannot disagree again.", "capability_tags": ["docs", "cli", "ux"], "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-20T10:25:53.000043Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "", "metadata": {}}, "foreman_note": null, "priority": "P2", "reasoning": "DOC-7 (decision-mode/handoff CLI surface undocumented) and DOC-6 (flags table incomplete) are complete, but the docs that landed still disagree with argparse: the run refuses to start without the flag while two README lines advertise a default. This is the first-touch experience — a new user's very first command fails — which is why it is filed rather than noted.", "source": null, "status": "pending", "type": "docs", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T10:25:53.000043Z"}
-{"id": "QA-CONSOLE-4", "title": "[P1] console POST /control/start answers {\"status\":\"started\"} but the session never ticks: update_tick() is called only from tests, never by the dashboard process", "description": "Dogfood use 2026-11-20 against the documented console (PTP_DASHBOARD_PORT=8791, header X-API-Key from .env). POST /control/start -> HTTP 200 {\"status\":\"started\",\"session_id\":\"default\"}. Follow-up reads: GET /status after 12s and again after 44s -> tick_count 0, tick_rate_avg 0.0, current_state 'idle', location 'Unknown', last_action_time null; GET /metrics -> total_ticks 0, total_commands 0, session_active true; GET /screenshots/latest -> 404. Server access log shows no error for those requests. Cause in code: DashboardSession.start() (src/dashboard/main.py:159-163) only flips flags — nothing spawns an emulator/AI loop; DashboardSession.update_tick() (src/dashboard/main.py:177) is the only writer of tick_count/location/last_action_time, and its ONLY call sites in the repo are in tests/test_dashboard.py:291-377 — the dashboard process itself never calls it. That is why the unit suite is green and the live console is dead: the tests exercise a method no production path invokes (phantom coverage), so 'tests pass' told nobody that Start does nothing. So the console's primary action returns success and then shows a permanently idle agent, and docs/api/dashboard.md:57 ('Start a fresh session') and :74 (sessions own a GameDatabase and ScreenshotManager) describe behaviour the process cannot perform.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) Make /control/start produce observable progress, OR make it fail honestly. Preferred: wire the session to the documented play loop so a started session updates tick_count, location and screenshots (cite the file:line of the loop you attach in worker_summary). 2) Proof: start the dashboard, POST /control/start, wait 20s, and record GET /status with tick_count > 0 and a non-'Unknown' location, plus GET /screenshots/latest returning an image (200 + bytes) instead of 404. Paste the exact curl commands and responses in worker_summary. 3) Negative proof: if the console is intentionally a remote-control surface only (no play loop), then change the response contract instead — return 409/501 with a message naming the required outside driver, and update docs/api/dashboard.md:57/:74 to match. Either way the state a user reads must never be 'running: true, ticks forever 0' with no error. 4) Add a test that a started session reaches tick_count >= 1 within a bounded wait, or asserts the explicit refusal — test path in worker_summary.", "capability_tags": ["console", "dashboard", "ux"], "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-20T10:25:53.000043Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "", "metadata": {}}, "foreman_note": null, "priority": "P1", "reasoning": "Found by using the console the way a real user would click Start: the API answers success, the UI state says running, and nothing ever happens. The dead end is structural (update_tick has no caller), so it cannot be a slow-boot race: 44s with zero ticks and no screenshot is conclusive.", "source": null, "status": "pending", "type": "bug", "updated_at": "2026-11-20T10:25:53.000043Z"}
